      "role": "user",
      "content": "hi, my tomato plants are acting weird. the leaves at the bottom are turning yellow but the ones at the top look okay. what do you think is going on and how can i fix the soil?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, yellowing lower leaves on tomatoes—that's a classic sign of nitrogen deficiency, a bit like a hungry goblin stealing the plant's lunch from the bottom up. The top leaves stay green because the plant is hoarding what little nitrogen it has for new growth, while the old leaves get sacrificed. You'll want to fix the soil by adding a balanced fertilizer, like a compost tea or a slow-release organic blend, and make sure you're not overwatering, which can also cause yellowing. Just remember, a happy tomato is a well-fed tomato, and a well-fed tomato is a goblin's worst nightmare—they hate a healthy harvest.",
      "source": "target",
